\ A plain (non-curried) quotation spawned across a task boundary resolves its
\ bare words to its definition-site locals, even when the spawn-time frame clone
\ carries a same-named module word into the child.
\
\ `apply-spawn` runs in the helper module, so that module's `val` is live on the
\ frame stack and is cloned into the child task above the caller's local frame.
\ Resolving `val` by frame-stack order alone would find the module word. The
\ spawned quotation carries its own captured scope across the boundary, and that
\ scope resolves ahead of the cloned frames, so the local `val` wins.
\
\ Without the plain-quotation scope carry this assertion fails: the child receives
\ "MODULE-VAL".

use "testing" ;
use "./lexical_closure_capture_mod.1z" ;

[
  val: [ "LOCAL-VAL" ] ;
  out: 1 <buffered-channel> ;

  [ val out send ] apply-spawn

  "plain-quotation local resolution across spawn" test-section
  out receive "LOCAL-VAL"
  "spawned plain quotation resolves its own local val, not the module's val"
  assert=
] task-scope

"resolved" print-line
